#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124341 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124532 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/id #uuid "a285ae3e-4f06-5e09-a06f-b2620be99888" :question/math-academy-id "e-5504" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which $\\lambda_1=-1,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ $\\lambda_3=0$ are eigenvalues, and\n![](images/28/28c1e8d1a466398336c357726d7f345a39e6ace73ab4b36147328b3f0a7313b9.png)\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n![](images/56/5611ead246c3e5a26d991603a68f3326b1c381d44eddd116316ca7ea6427fdd8.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1},$ $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ $\\lambda_3=0,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n![](images/65/65491fb3de59e45bffb4774aca31cc25f20f555f18ffafc8d56221c689520380.png)\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1},$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_3=0.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n![](images/37/3798d5e0b3305070161e2df70796a20a8fcd1e11f11cecdc94d59db520582ca3.png)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n![](images/14/1448624dbf0dbb227d5e2a3f082dbda389c521d905b1eee1aafe9a2226e25cfd.png)\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=0,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124342 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124533 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "3dd8d6e6-76a2-591c-90a5-86074539686b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5505" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n![](images/62/62f202b2fdbe406e4785cc4acf7f2b9aaa09980bc894340716fbbe5600fb8ae9.png)\nDetermine whether $A=PDP^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $\\mathbb R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )([{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +2]-12) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(\\lambda -5)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=4,$ $\\lambda_2=5,$ and $\\lambda_3=-2.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n![](images/2f/2f943468de2124874c2d806db25c6ca91a2df9a74902af95220e4974e5ef483b.png)\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible  diagonal matrix is\n\n![](images/6f/6f73d40195a91530d761d966db886e9ad2d87d7fc1059a4112a0a2701ce6a72f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124343 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124534 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "b3307ae8-eb7d-533a-aef2-c7f50dea07e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5506" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n![](images/7d/7d992806d0d5b90b65030f8c0080f105f67fbb7b4d3d807c3a719307e0c31e40.png)\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP,$ find the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are  \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=-2, \\quad$ $\\lambda_2=-1, \\quad$ $\\lambda_3=2.$ \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_1=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 14\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{8}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=x_3.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/fd/fdf2311a1010ed8e8f25623328171687a63ea5da80f6e18a1a8dfbff9e5fae42.png)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_3=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/34/34e8934a56885ec189cc1819b1336f5b52b7708a092233e3c20e0960218cad10.png)\n\nFinally, ![](images/ec/ec378af1062dda61e89d060d565ef70bf7593a723bd3cba78dfd86f0d027dc5d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124385 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124578 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "8d2aff64-bf98-560b-867d-6b47ccc7dbe7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7330" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca47231d1f55afd5641f3924cae2252af74467ba29adbbdb51832cbd4cf06663.png) \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=2,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ and $\\lambda_3=0.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=2,\\quad$ $\\lambda_2=1, \\quad$ $\\lambda_3=0.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](images/b8/b88fd696293e4151a71ca12d25cb56821ba034e07a876777fa30d5d6f6b9c4c3.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](images/38/38941ad5615365437a1aee93f085b33d6a240c5a61ce8e0f0a10acb465de0f18.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_3=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](images/a3/a3ea884b0f6ba11582eb72e6cffcc871a144e55c446aaa304e1c7f9face13c7b.png)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](images/cc/cc87c4f07116bf301d115ffeb00e81f9cd25d21cb40f61fdbc310f86557a61b4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124578 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ae4b6c0-9ac5-530e-a84b-630305f7709b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269486} {:answer/id #uuid "4210454b-1b57-5202-b24c-d9ba774f094c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269863} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa01c8c-fa5e-5db3-9890-98afc410a0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270630} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b21af7-648d-570d-aab4-7958bcae5273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270762} {:answer/id #uuid "fb277ce2-2359-57d8-9955-89059a164bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ae4b6c0-9ac5-530e-a84b-630305f7709b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269486} :answer-field/id #uuid "a98d67e2-0ccf-58ae-aa10-005055ae0213" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d8196086-ee7b-5323-b641-b0ce439cc41f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140262" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=9.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=9.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-9I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124532 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f767944-0ec4-5b51-b0ff-932c44740c6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123207} {:answer/id #uuid "40729a7b-eec0-5002-86cf-545e757bf6c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123208} {:answer/id #uuid "528da589-9cf4-5fb2-bde6-eef70784ff29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123209} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce7a52b-a17b-50d3-bc96-430767a7995d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123210} {:answer/id #uuid "7da1774d-e6f8-5388-abe4-d072ffb222d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123211}] :answer-field/id #uuid "883bc44f-5335-5507-b72b-47a96f7fe8f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123206}] :question/id #uuid "17cb4954-c3d0-565d-94a6-8a00df11ece9" :question/math-academy-id "q-37806" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which $λ_{1} =-3$, $λ_{2} = 1$, $λ_{3} =-1$ are eigenvalues, and\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nare the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = A$, where $D$ is a diagonal matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124533 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87175b0a-99ed-5f49-a7c0-8adc200a91cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123249} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe083d5-d528-507e-b77c-4a2e3ceb765d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123250} {:answer/id #uuid "cc855a26-5166-51cd-ab18-7c57fb9e4293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123251} {:answer/id #uuid "2ba312d5-848e-570e-96ae-4ccd284f1e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123252} {:answer/id #uuid "33ab1655-07bf-5646-a049-16964d89eca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123253}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d83d46e9-6f67-5e08-a7f7-2fb0653514c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123248} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b039043-81b4-5fc5-8708-7fbf237a4ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382067} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0942e1-d697-5f1d-ad8d-e9b6258b9eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382068} {:answer/id #uuid "6cdae987-e461-5f61-b5d9-45cafc99eb3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382069} {:answer/id #uuid "972e4d42-5535-532c-a9ea-1b24b7a4b9fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382070} {:answer/id #uuid "99def5b0-3eb9-5fe1-a95a-3aba50761daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c0942e1-d697-5f1d-ad8d-e9b6258b9eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382068} :answer-field/id #uuid "6099f13f-2d83-545c-81d5-a7792b6fc7be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be37aea9-9ebb-51c3-9045-5fceee548409" :question/math-academy-id "q-37843" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -1 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124533 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89f3e2af-1d0c-5ede-94d8-b2f1171885f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395873} {:answer/id #uuid "aea3c10a-5a5b-5b56-ac38-0531c16322a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395874} {:answer/id #uuid "11ba3c2a-7e4a-5f75-b416-64d7e2c0bb58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395875} {:answer/id #uuid "842abd30-146b-5529-8174-62765bece360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395876} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e2d4db-4322-5304-a102-6991fb8129e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aea3c10a-5a5b-5b56-ac38-0531c16322a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395874} :answer-field/id #uuid "72ff45fc-3165-5e0a-8ffa-750ac771413e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21c29510-ffed-5744-82ad-55435a6f5d16" :question/math-academy-id "q-37846" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -9-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-16) & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15) & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -5) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-9,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ Note that all $\\lambda $'s are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial.\n\nSince all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124534 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d113fd3-c71b-5d9e-955d-e46183c3748e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382074} {:answer/id #uuid "39618bc3-2b12-586f-bdd9-ef421e79b6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382075} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f72f3d-98cf-5349-9c0a-5e49707de3c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382076} {:answer/id #uuid "04d7ca2c-b383-5d9e-84b4-63fd717e6dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382077} {:answer/id #uuid "708eb7f4-bd39-5814-8802-8b791bc777c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3f72f3d-98cf-5349-9c0a-5e49707de3c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382076} :answer-field/id #uuid "271a716c-d9a8-5efe-8102-cf2499e7bd78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f806fd6-4180-5914-82ca-9edb27806cc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37848" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 12 & -10 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & a & -2 \\\\ -1 & b & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $(a+b)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$ Computing $A-6I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{2}I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 12 & -10 & -6\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 12 & -10 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -10 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 14 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 14 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st  and the $2$nd ones). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get  ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $(a+b)^{2}=(0+1)^{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124578 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a5e97c5-f73a-5dc7-aca2-86493283b121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351803} {:answer/id #uuid "259df9a5-e148-52b1-8c76-e5f3797156f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351913} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0b1af0-e162-51c8-a699-561daf399c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352507} {:answer/id #uuid "90343dd9-95c9-5f79-b6c6-c97b7b8b1870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352816} {:answer/id #uuid "aeab5e76-18bb-5fe4-8750-1b5fd4ac3465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a5e97c5-f73a-5dc7-aca2-86493283b121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351803} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7b7eafb-592d-5378-aed9-4f494837b137" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b7c3932-3369-5860-a2cf-645dd0b0d0ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-84611" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}]}
